<header><p class="enc-kicker">Institution</p><h1 class="enc-title">NATO</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1949">1949</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as North Atlantic Treaty Organization</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The North Atlantic Treaty Organization is a military alliance founded by the North Atlantic Treaty, signed in Washington on 4 April 1949 by twelve countries of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-america">North America</a> and western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>, including <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/canada">Canada</a>. Its members pledge that an armed attack on one is an attack on all. Created to deter the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soviet-union">Soviet Union</a>, it expanded into central and eastern Europe after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cold-war">Cold War</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/nato.jpg" alt="NATO" width="320" height="161" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:NATO_OTAN_landscape_logo.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Armenia ratified the Treaty on Conventional Armed Forces in Europe in 1992, acceded to the Nuclear Non-Proliferation Treaty in 1993, joined the Collective Security Treaty Organisation and sent troops to <b>NATO</b>'s KFOR in Kosovo in 2004; Russia keeps a base at Gyumri as a deterrent against Turkey.</blockquote><a href="/empire/armenia">Armenia · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>By 2021 the armed forces had 126,000 active personnel and 17,000 paramilitary troops, a defence budget of $2.24 billion in 2020, 5.4 percent of GDP, and an arms industry of its own; Azerbaijan cooperates with <b>NATO</b> through the Partnership for Peace and sent peacekeepers to Iraq and Afghanistan.</blockquote><a href="/empire/azerbaijan">Azerbaijan · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>A founding member of <b>NATO</b> in 1949, Canada fought in the Korean War, joined the United States in the North American Air Defence Command in 1958, took part in many United Nations peacekeeping missions, and fought in Afghanistan from 2001.</blockquote><a href="/empire/canada">Canada · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>West Germany joined <b>NATO</b> in 1955, faced the Berlin Wall from 1961 and eased tensions with the East through Willy Brandt's Ostpolitik in the late 1960s.</blockquote><a href="/empire/federal-republic-of-germany">Federal Republic of Germany · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>Georgia sent up to 2,000 soldiers to Iraq and 1,560 to Afghanistan in 2013, the largest non-<b>NATO</b> contingent, where 32 died.</blockquote><a href="/empire/georgia">Georgia · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>After 1945 Luxembourg introduced conscription for a time, sent volunteers to fight in Korea alongside the Belgian battalion and has since kept a small professional army within <b>NATO</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/grand-duchy-of-luxembourg">Grand Duchy of Luxembourg · Military</a></li></ul></section>
